<div class="container-fluid py-3 py-lg-4">
    <section class="topic-hero mb-4">
        <h1 class="h2 fw-bold mb-2">1.3 What are Microservices?</h1>
        <p class="mb-2">A beginner-friendly explanation of microservices, how they differ from monolithic systems, and when they make sense in real projects.</p>
        <a href="/Microservices/Section/course-introduction" class="btn btn-sm btn-light mt-2">← Back to Microservices with ASP.NET Core – Complete Syllabus</a>
    </section>

    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Friendly Introduction</h2>
                <p>When developers first hear the word <strong>microservices</strong>, it can sound like a complex architecture meant only for big tech companies. But the idea is actually simple: instead of building one giant application, you split the system into smaller services, where each service handles one clear business capability.</p>
                <p class="mb-0">Think of it as moving from a single large kitchen where everyone works in one crowded space, to specialized stations where each team handles one part efficiently. That is the spirit of microservices.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">In this lesson, we will discuss</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>What microservices are and their key characteristics.</li>
                    <li>How microservices differ from monolithic architecture.</li>
                    <li>A step-by-step way to understand service boundaries.</li>
                    <li>An e-commerce mini case study.</li>
                    <li>Benefits, challenges, and practical best practices.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Step-by-Step Understanding of Microservices</h2>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 1: Start from business capabilities</h3>
                <p>Identify business functions like catalog, cart, payment, shipping, and notifications. In microservices, each function can become a separate service with its own logic.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 2: Make each service independently deployable</h3>
                <p>One important characteristic is autonomy. If you update the Catalog Service, you should not be forced to redeploy the entire application.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 3: Prefer loose communication</h3>
                <p>Services usually communicate through HTTP APIs or asynchronous messaging (for example, events published to a broker). This avoids tight coupling between teams.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 4: Keep data ownership clear</h3>
                <p>In a true microservices architecture, each service owns its data. Sharing one database across all services can quickly create the same coupling issues as a monolith.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 5: Design for failure and monitoring</h3>
                <p class="mb-0">Distributed systems fail in different ways. So, retries, circuit breakers, logs, metrics, and tracing are not optional—they are part of the architecture.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Core Characteristics of Microservices</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Microservices = Small, independent services
  + Business-focused boundaries
  + Independent deployment
  + Decentralized data ownership
  + API/Event-based communication
  + Fault isolation and observability</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Real-World Example: E-commerce Platform</h2>
                <p>An online shopping company starts with a monolithic application. As traffic grows, checkout becomes slow during sale days. The team moves to microservices:</p>
                <ul class="list-tight">
                    <li><strong>Catalog Service</strong> handles products and search.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cart Service</strong> manages user carts.</li>
                    <li><strong>Order Service</strong> creates and tracks orders.</li>
                    <li><strong>Payment Service</strong> integrates with payment providers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Notification Service</strong> sends email/SMS updates.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mb-0">Now the company can scale only Payment and Order services during peak traffic, release catalog updates quickly, and isolate failures so one problem does not break the entire shopping flow.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Benefits / Advantages</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>Independent deployment for faster releases.</li>
                    <li>Better scaling by service, not by full application.</li>
                    <li>Fault isolation improves reliability.</li>
                    <li>Teams can work in parallel with clear ownership.</li>
                    <li>Technology flexibility where needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Challenges / Limitations</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>Distributed debugging is harder than monolith debugging.</li>
                    <li>Requires stronger DevOps and monitoring maturity.</li>
                    <li>Network calls introduce latency and partial failures.</li>
                    <li>Data consistency across services needs careful design.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="callout callout-warning mt-3 mb-0">
                    Microservices are powerful, but not always the first best choice. For small teams or early MVPs, a modular monolith is often faster and safer.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Microservices vs Monolithic Architecture</h2>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-bordered comparison-table align-middle mb-0">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Monolith</th>
                                <th>Microservices</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Deployment</td>
                                <td>Single unit deployment</td>
                                <td>Each service deploys independently</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Scaling</td>
                                <td>Scale whole application</td>
                                <td>Scale only the hot services</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Codebase</td>
                                <td>Single large codebase</td>
                                <td>Multiple smaller codebases/services</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Failure impact</td>
                                <td>One issue can affect entire app</td>
                                <td>Faults can be isolated</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Operational complexity</td>
                                <td>Lower initially</td>
                                <td>Higher (monitoring, tracing, deployment)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Best Practices, Tips, and Common Mistakes</h2>
                <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Best Practices</h3>
                <ul class="list-tight">
                    <li>Design service boundaries around business domains, not technical layers.</li>
                    <li>Introduce centralized logging and distributed tracing early.</li>
                    <li>Use API versioning and backward-compatible changes.</li>
                    <li>Automate testing and CI/CD per service.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="h6 fw-bold mt-3">Common Mistakes</h3>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>Breaking services too small too early (“nano-services” problem).</li>
                    <li>Using shared database tables across services.</li>
                    <li>Ignoring resilience patterns (timeouts, retries, fallback).</li>
                    <li>Choosing microservices without a real business reason.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">Microservices are an architectural style where applications are built as a collection of small, business-focused, independently deployable services. They can dramatically improve scalability and release speed, but they also introduce operational complexity. The best approach is to choose microservices when your product scale, team structure, and business needs actually demand them.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Interview Questions and Answers</h2>
                <div class="accordion" id="microservicesQaAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq1"><button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="msa1">1) What are microservices in simple terms?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="msq1" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Microservices are a way of building an application as multiple small services, where each service handles one business function and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq2"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa2">2) How are microservices different from a monolithic architecture?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq2" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">A monolith is one deployable unit with tightly connected modules. Microservices split the system into independently deployable services that communicate over APIs or events.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq3"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa3">3) Why do teams prefer microservices for large products?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq3" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Because teams can release features faster, scale high-traffic areas independently, and isolate failures. This is useful for large products with many parallel teams.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq4"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa4">4) Can we use one shared database for all microservices?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq4" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Technically possible, but not recommended. Shared databases create tight coupling and reduce team independence. A better pattern is database per service where practical.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq5"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa5">5) What is one practical challenge in microservices?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq5" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Observability is a major challenge. Since requests travel across multiple services, you need centralized logging, correlation IDs, and distributed tracing to troubleshoot efficiently.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq6"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa6">6) Give one e-commerce example where microservices help.</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa6" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq6" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">During a flash sale, Order and Payment services can be scaled independently without scaling Catalog or Notification services, improving cost and performance.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="msq7"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#msa7" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="msa7">7) Are microservices always better than monoliths?</button></h2>
                        <div id="msa7" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="msq7" data-bs-parent="#microservicesQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">No. For small or early-stage projects, monoliths are often simpler and faster. Microservices are better when scale, team autonomy, and independent deployment become necessary.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
